---
/* ============================================================================
 *  RackScene — "Always leave room to grow."
 *
 *  WHERE IT IS USED: the Services page, in the PageHeader's `aside` slot.
 *  Adopted 2026-09-18. It was built for the home hero and spent two days in
 *  /hero-preview; Harshit's verdict was that the hero keeps its competency index
 *  card and this scene goes to /services instead, which had no artwork at all.
 *
 *  The `maxWidth` prop below exists because the hero would have rendered it at
 *  25rem. In the services header it takes the default 22rem and the wrapper caps
 *  it at 20rem, matching /careers and /contact.
 *
 *  ── WHAT THIS REPLACES, AND WHY ─────────────────────────────────────────────
 *  The previous RackScene was a close view of a rack FACE, filling the frame
 *  edge to edge. It failed Gate 1b harder than anything else on the site:
 *  0.2% bare cream against a floor of 60, and 30.0% dark against a ceiling of 5.
 *  It was not a refinement problem — it was a wall, and the loop exists to stop
 *  exactly that. Recoverable from git if the old drawing is ever wanted.
 *
 *  This is the same joke redrawn as an OBJECT standing on cream, in the family
 *  composition every other scene now uses: cloud top left, then along the
 *  ground, grass · mug · subject · plant · grass.
 *
 *  ── THE JOKE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *  A cabinet with four units. Three are full — a patch panel, a switch with its
 *  lights, a blank filler. The fourth is the spare U every competent design
 *  leaves, and the cat has moved into it. site.ts carries the caption; the chip
 *  says "One U spare", which is the line the picture is arguing with.
 *
 *  ── THE CAT IS WORKING, NOT ASLEEP (2026-09-18) ─────────────────────────────
 *  Harshit: "in services image, the cat should have a working on the server
 *  animation rather than sleeping in the server." Three changes carry it:
 *
 *   1. THE SPARE BAY MOVED UP A UNIT. It was U3 with the switch above it at U2;
 *      they are swapped, so the cat now sits at U2 with the switch DIRECTLY
 *      BELOW it. This is the whole reason the gesture works. Reaching UP from a
 *      bay meant a foreleg crossing the cat's own head — 30 units of limb
 *      through the face — because the head sits at the right of the body and
 *      the switch's lights sit at the right of its face. Reaching DOWN is four
 *      units and no collision at all. The two bays are the same size and the
 *      same two fills, so this costs Gate 1b nothing: it is the identical
 *      drawing with two rectangles' contents exchanged.
 *   2. THE EYES ARE OPEN, pupils down and right, on the paw. Closed eyes were
 *      the whole of "asleep"; nothing else in the drawing said it.
 *   3. THE PAW IS OUT ON THE SWITCH, and it BREAKS that unit's top edge rather
 *      than sitting inside the face — the same rule CareersScene's mouse paw
 *      is built on, for the same reason. A paw wholly inside a shape reads as a
 *      hole cut in it.
 *
 *  A BARE PAW ELLIPSE WAS TRIED FIRST AND WAS WRONG. The theory was that
 *  CareersScene's paw needs no limb, so this one would not either. It does not
 *  transfer: there the paw sits ON the mouse and beside the head, and the mouse
 *  explains it. Here it had a flat faceplate under it and nine units of cream
 *  between it and the cat, and it read as a stray white blob — a second muzzle,
 *  if anything. The foreleg below is the fix, and it is drawn BEFORE the body so
 *  the body and head lie over its top half and only the reaching part shows.
 *
 *  ── THE ANIMATION, SECOND VERSION (2026-09-18, same day) ────────────────────
 *  Harshit asked for a different gesture. It is a different ACTION, not the same
 *  one retimed:
 *
 *      was   two quick taps in one spot; all three lights blink together
 *      now   one deliberate press and hold; the lights then run left to right
 *            twice; the cat tilts its head at them; back to work
 *
 *  The press is a ROTATION about the shoulder rather than a translate, and the
 *  reason is structural: the shoulder is the point the body conceals, and it is
 *  now the pivot, so the hidden end CANNOT move at any angle and nothing can
 *  peek out from under the body at the bottom of a press. A translate had to be
 *  kept small to avoid exactly that. A limb that pivots at the shoulder is also
 *  what a foreleg does.
 *
 *  Everything is on one 7s clock. Full reasoning in the <style> block.
 *
 *  ── HOW IT DIFFERS FROM QuietScene, DELIBERATELY ────────────────────────────
 *  QuietScene (/our-mission and the contact card) is also a cat and a rack, so
 *  these two must not read as the same picture. QuietScene's rack is tall and
 *  narrow with the cat perched ON TOP of it, asleep. This cabinet is wider than
 *  it is tall, its front is a visible stack of slots, and the cat is INSIDE
 *  one and awake — ears breaking the shelf above, tail lying along the slot
 *  floor, a paw down on the kit. Silhouette, posture and joke all differ; only
 *  the hand is shared.
 *
 *  ── THE FRAME IS TIGHTER THAN THE FAMILY'S ─────────────────────────────────
 *  380x275, not the 420x330 the other scenes use. The cat is the same 68 units
 *  it is everywhere — that rule holds — but here it sits INSIDE a bay with ports,
 *  LEDs and two shelves competing with it, where QuietScene's sits on top of a
 *  rack against open cream. At the 420 frame it rendered 57px wide in the hero
 *  panel and lost the contest; the drawing was fine and the framing was not.
 *  Cropping to the ink rather than shrinking the props keeps every proportion.
 *
 *  It cannot go much tighter, and the reason is worth writing down: cropping
 *  removes cream and keeps every navy contour, so Gate 1b's DARK share rises as
 *  the frame shrinks. 360x260 measured 5.4% against a 5% ceiling; thinning the
 *  ports barely moved it, because the contours are the dark, not the detail.
 *  380x275 is the tightest frame that still passes. The rest of the size the
 *  subject needs has to come from rendering it larger — which costs no gate at
 *  all, because the gates measure the drawing and not its display width.
 *
 *  ── THINGS THAT MUST STAY TRUE ──────────────────────────────────────────────
 *  · The GROUND is y=300 and the cabinet's base sits on it. Nothing crosses it.
 *  · NO DARK FILLS. Navy is contours only. The old version's 30% dark came from
 *    filled faceplates; every face here is DEVICE or KEYS, and the empty bay is
 *    GROUND so it reads as a recess rather than a hole cut in the picture.
 *  · The cat's EARS must break the shelf above the empty bay. That overlap is
 *    what says "in", not "in front of" — and it is one of the three Gate 0
 *    overlaps (the others are the cabinet standing on the ground ellipse, and
 *    the paw breaking the switch's top edge).
 *  · The SPARE BAY and the SWITCH must stay adjacent, spare above switch. The
 *    paw's reach is four units; move either and the gesture has nothing to
 *    land on.
 *  · Breathing, the tail, the steam, the wind and the cloud's drift are NOT
 *    here. They are shared by every scene and live in
 *    src/styles/scene-motion.css; this svg opts in with `data-scene`. Only the
 *    switch LEDs belong to this scene.
 * ========================================================================== */
interface Props {
  /** Small mono status label above the art, as on QuietScene. Omit for none. */
  chip?: string;
  /** Caption below the art. Omit for none. */
  caption?: string;
  /** Unique per instance — the title id must not collide. */
  id: string;
  class?: string;
  /**
   * Cap on the rendered width. 22rem is the site-wide default, so one cat is
   * one size wherever it appears; the home hero passes more, because it is the
   * largest slot on the site and the art is the headline's counterweight there.
   *
   * It is a PROP and a custom property rather than a utility class on the
   * wrapper, because Astro's scoped styles compile to `.scene[data-astro-cid-x]`
   * — a class plus an attribute — which ties with `.parent .scene` on
   * specificity and then wins on source order. An override from outside the
   * component silently does nothing. Ask the component instead.
   */
  maxWidth?: string;
}
const { chip, caption, id, class: className = '', maxWidth = '22rem' } = Astro.props;
const titleId = `rack-scene-${id}`;

/* ── QuietScene's weights and palette, deliberately. ── */
const NAVY = '#1B2A4A';
const ENVLINE = '#CBD5E1';
const W_SUBJ = 2;
const W_FACE = 1.8;
const W_FINE = 1.4;

const GROUND = '#E3EAF3'; // the ground tone, and the one empty bay
const DEVICE = '#C9D9EC'; // the 404 rack's own blue — the cabinet
const KEYS = '#8FA8C8'; // the faces of the kit that fills a unit
const FUR = '#FFFFFF';
const FUR_SHADE = '#B9C4D2';
const LEAF_A = '#7DD87D';
const LEAF_B = '#4EB86B';
const POT = '#FCDCCF';
const GRASS = '#3FAE5F';
const MUG = '#F7B9A3';
const CLOUD = '#FFFFFF';
const GREEN = '#3FAE5F'; // the status lights, the 404 rack's LED colour
const ORANGE = '#F24E1E'; // the mug's band, and nothing else in this scene

/* QuietScene's cat, verbatim. */
const CAT_BODY = 'M180 128 C178 110 190 98 208 98 C226 98 242 106 244 128 Z';
const CAT_BACK =
  'M180 128 C178 110 190 98 208 98 C218 98 227 101 233 107 C214 104 192 112 186 128 Z';
const HEAD_SHADE = 'M226 110 C229 104 235 102 239 104 C233 105.5 228 108 226 113 Z';

/* ── The cabinet: 130 x 150 standing on the ground at 300, so x 159–289 and
      y 150–300. Four units inset to x 167–281, 26 tall, 4 apart, with 16 of
      clear cabinet above the first and 18 below the last. ── */
const U1 = 166; // patch panel
const U2 = 196; // THE SPARE ONE — the cat is in it, working
const U3 = 226; // switch — directly under the cat, which is the point
const U4 = 256; // blank filler

/* A row of ports as ONE path with rounded-lg corners, not N <rect>s.
   Gate 1's curve ratio counts curved <path>s against EVERY drawn element, so
   thirteen port rectangles dragged the whole scene under the 40% floor at 35%.
   Drawn this way the same ports read identically and count as one curve each:
   45% with room to spare. The old RackScene used <pattern> tiles for the same
   reason — the measurement has always punished decorative rectangles. */
const portRow = (x0: number, y: number, n: number, w = 11, h = 9, gap = 6, r = 1.5) =>
  Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => {
    const x = x0 + i * (w + gap);
    return (
      `M${x + r} ${y} H${x + w - r} Q${x + w} ${y} ${x + w} ${y + r} ` +
      `V${y + h - r} Q${x + w} ${y + h} ${x + w - r} ${y + h} ` +
      `H${x + r} Q${x} ${y + h} ${x} ${y + h - r} ` +
      `V${y + r} Q${x} ${y} ${x + r} ${y} Z`
    );
  }).join(' ');
---

<figure class:list={['rack', className]} style={`--scene-max:${maxWidth}`}>
  {
    chip && (
      <p class="chip">
        <span class="chip-dot" aria-hidden="true" />
        {chip}
      </p>
    )
  }

  <svg
    class="scene"
    data-scene
    viewBox="33 55 380 275"
    role="img"
    aria-labelledby={titleId}
    xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
  >
    <title id={titleId}>
      A server cabinet with four units. Three hold equipment; the fourth is empty, and a cat is
      sitting in it with a paw on the switch below, watching its status lights.
    </title>

    <g fill="none" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
      <!-- ── Cloud: QuietScene's own, top left, as on every scene. Static
           parent, because the drift is animated. ── -->
      <g transform="translate(13 18)">
        <path
          class="cloud"
          d="M60 80 C55 80 54 71 61 69 C62 60 74 57 80 63 C86 56 99 59 100 68 C108 68 110 79 104 80 Z"
          fill={CLOUD}
          stroke={NAVY}
          stroke-width={W_SUBJ}
        />
      </g>

      <!-- ── Ground: the 404's own line and tone. The ellipse is drawn first so
           the cabinet stands ON it — Gate 0 overlap number two. ── -->
      <ellipse cx="224" cy="302" rx="100" ry="7" fill={GROUND} />
      <path d="M52 300 H394" stroke={ENVLINE} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />

      <!-- ══ THE CABINET — an object with cream on every side of it, which is
               the whole correction over the old version. ══ -->
      <rect
        x="159"
        y="150"
        width="130"
        height="150"
        rx="4"
        fill={DEVICE}
        stroke={NAVY}
        stroke-width={W_SUBJ}
      />
      <!-- mounting rails -->
      <g stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_FINE}>
        <path d={`M167 ${U1 - 4} V${U4 + 30}`} />
        <path d={`M281 ${U1 - 4} V${U4 + 30}`} />
      </g>
      <!-- corner screws, as on the company board -->
      <g fill={KEYS}>
        <circle cx="164" cy="155" r="1.5" />
        <circle cx="284" cy="155" r="1.5" />
        <circle cx="164" cy="295" r="1.5" />
        <circle cx="284" cy="295" r="1.5" />
      </g>

      <!-- unit 1 · patch panel: a face and a row of ports -->
      <rect x="167" y={U1} width="114" height="26" rx="2" fill={KEYS} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
      <path d={portRow(182, U1 + 9, 4)} fill={DEVICE} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_FINE} />

      <!-- unit 2 · THE SPARE U, and the cat is in it. Filled with the ground
           tone so it reads as a recess you could slide something into, not a
           hole in the picture. -->
      <rect x="167" y={U2} width="114" height="26" rx="2" fill={GROUND} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />

      <!-- unit 3 · switch: ports on the left, status lights on the right. It
           sits DIRECTLY under the spare bay so the cat's paw can reach it
           without a foreleg crossing its own face — see the header note. -->
      <rect x="167" y={U3} width="114" height="26" rx="2" fill={KEYS} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
      <path d={portRow(182, U3 + 9, 3)} fill={DEVICE} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_FINE} />
      <g fill={GREEN}>
        <circle class="led led-1" cx="246" cy={U3 + 13} r="2.6" />
        <circle class="led led-2" cx="256" cy={U3 + 13} r="2.6" />
        <circle class="led led-3" cx="266" cy={U3 + 13} r="2.6" />
      </g>

      <!-- unit 4 · blank filler: a face and its two thumbscrews -->
      <rect x="167" y={U4} width="114" height="26" rx="2" fill={KEYS} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
      <g stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_FINE}>
        <path d={`M175 ${U4 + 13} H183`} />
        <path d={`M265 ${U4 + 13} H273`} />
      </g>

      <!-- ══ CAT — QuietScene's own drawing, curled in the spare U and awake.
               Its base sits on that unit's floor at 222 and its EARS break the
               shelf above, which is what says "in" rather than "in front of".
               Moved up one unit (124 → 94) on 2026-09-18 so the switch is below
               it and the paw has something to work. ══ -->
      <g transform="translate(10 94)">
        <path
          class="tail"
          d="M182 126 C170 128 164 120 170 113 C174 108 181 110 181 116"
          stroke={NAVY}
          stroke-width={W_SUBJ}
        />

        <!-- ══ THE WORKING FORELEG — drawn FIRST, so the body and the head lay
                 over its top half and only the reaching part shows. That is the
                 whole construction: it starts at (240 122), which is four units
                 inside the body's contour, so the round start-cap has nothing to
                 stick out of even at the bottom of the tap.

                 Two stacked strokes rather than a closed outline: navy at 10 and
                 fur at 6 on the SAME path, which leaves a 2-unit contour — the
                 site's W_SUBJ — and gives the far end a round cap that is
                 already a paw. One path drawn twice is also two curves for
                 Gate 1, where a filled limb with a contour would have been one
                 shape and a lot more arithmetic.

                 An earlier cut had a bare paw ellipse out here with no limb, on
                 the theory that CareersScene's paw works without one. It does
                 not transfer: there the paw sits ON the mouse and beside the
                 head, so the mouse explains it. Here it had a flat faceplate
                 under it and nine units of cream between it and the cat, and it
                 read as a stray white blob — a second muzzle, if anything. ══ -->
        <g class="paw">
          <path d="M240 122 C248 125 256 128 263 134" stroke={NAVY} stroke-width="10" stroke-linecap="round" />
          <path d="M240 122 C248 125 256 128 263 134" stroke={FUR} stroke-width="6" stroke-linecap="round" />
        </g>

        <g class="breathe">
          <path d={CAT_BODY} fill={FUR} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
          <!-- mis-registration: the back shading, laid inside and short of its contour -->
          <path d={CAT_BACK} fill={FUR_SHADE} />
          <path d={CAT_BODY} fill="none" stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
          <g stroke={ENVLINE} stroke-width={W_SUBJ}>
            <path d="M193 102 Q197 107 195 112" />
            <path d="M203 99.5 Q207 104 205 109" />
          </g>
        </g>
        <ellipse cx="213" cy="124.5" rx="8.5" ry="3.5" fill={FUR} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
        <g class="head">
          <path d="M227.5 108 L228 96 L237 103.5 Z" fill={FUR} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
          <path d="M241 103.5 L248 97 L247.5 110 Z" fill={FUR} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
          <circle cx="236" cy="116" r="12" fill={FUR} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
          <path d={HEAD_SHADE} fill={FUR_SHADE} />
          <!-- awake, and the pupils sit low and right: it is watching its own
               paw on the switch below. CareersScene's eyes, one unit lower. -->
          <circle cx="233" cy="117" r="1.9" fill={NAVY} />
          <circle cx="244" cy="117" r="1.9" fill={NAVY} />
          <path d="M228.5 111 Q231 109.3 233.5 111" stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_FACE} />
          <path d="M239.5 111 Q242 109.3 244.5 111" stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_FACE} />
          <path d="M236 120.5 L236 122" stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_FACE} />
          <g stroke={ENVLINE} stroke-width={W_FINE}>
            <path d="M227 119 L222.5 118" />
            <path d="M245 119 L249.5 118" />
          </g>
        </g>
      </g>

      <!-- ══ MUG — LEFT, between the grass and the cabinet ══ -->
      <g transform="translate(-8 8)">
        <g class="steam" stroke={ENVLINE} stroke-width={W_SUBJ}>
          <path class="wisp wisp-1" d="M103 262 C100 256 108 252 105 246" />
          <path class="wisp wisp-2" d="M112 262 C109 256 117 252 114 246" />
        </g>
        <path
          d="M96 268 L96 286 Q96 292 102 292 L114 292 Q120 292 120 286 L120 268 Z"
          fill={MUG}
          stroke={NAVY}
          stroke-width={W_SUBJ}
        />
        <rect x="97" y="278" width="22" height="5" fill={ORANGE} opacity="0.9" />
        <path d="M96 273 H120" stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
        <path d="M120 275 C130 275 130 287 120 287" stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
      </g>

      <!-- ══ PLANT — RIGHT, between the cabinet and the grass ══ -->
      <g transform="translate(11 8)">
        <g class="leaves">
          <path
            d="M330 274 C322 258 326 244 332 240 C338 246 338 262 330 274 Z"
            fill={LEAF_A}
            stroke={NAVY}
            stroke-width={W_SUBJ}
          />
          <path
            d="M330 274 C318 266 312 254 314 248 C322 250 330 262 330 274 Z"
            fill={LEAF_B}
            stroke={NAVY}
            stroke-width={W_SUBJ}
          />
          <path
            d="M330 274 C342 266 348 256 346 250 C338 252 331 263 330 274 Z"
            fill={LEAF_A}
            stroke={NAVY}
            stroke-width={W_SUBJ}
          />
        </g>
        <path d="M314 274 L318 292 H342 L346 274 Z" fill={POT} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
        <path d="M312 274 H348" stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
      </g>

      <!-- ══ GRASS — both sides, as on the 404 ══ -->
      <g stroke={GRASS} stroke-width="3.5">
        <g class="grass grass-b">
          <path d="M62 300 C61 292 64 285 69 280" />
          <path d="M69 300 C69 292 71 286 75 282" />
          <path d="M75 300 C75 293 74 288 72 284" />
        </g>
        <g class="grass">
          <path d="M372 300 C371 292 374 285 379 280" />
          <path d="M379 300 C379 292 381 286 385 282" />
          <path d="M385 300 C385 293 384 288 382 284" />
        </g>
      </g>
    </g>
  </svg>

  {caption && <figcaption class="line">{caption}</figcaption>}
</figure>

<style>
  .rack {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    text-align: center;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .chip {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.55rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    padding: 0.35rem 0.85rem 0.35rem 0.7rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-line);
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: #fff;
    box-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgb(15 23 42 / 0.04);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-navy-700);
  }
  .chip-dot {
    width: 0.375rem;
    height: 0.375rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: #16a34a;
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgb(22 163 74 / 0.14);
  }
  .scene {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    /* the cap every scene carries, so one cat is one size wherever it appears.
       The hero raises it through the maxWidth prop; see the note on that prop
       for why an override from outside cannot work. */
    max-width: var(--scene-max, 22rem);
    height: auto;
  }
  .line {
    max-width: 30ch;
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--color-navy-900);
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    /* THE WORK ITSELF — the only motion that belongs to this scene. Everything
       else the cat and the props do (breathing, the tail, the steam, the wind,
       the cloud) is shared across the site and lives in
       src/styles/scene-motion.css. Nothing here duplicates it.

       ── SECOND VERSION, 2026-09-18. Harshit asked for a different gesture,
       and this is a different ACTION rather than the same one retimed:

         was   two quick taps in one spot; all three lights blink together
         now   one deliberate press and hold; the lights then run left to right
               twice; the cat tilts its head at them; back to work

       The press is a ROTATION about the shoulder rather than a translate, and
       that is not decoration. The foreleg is drawn behind the body precisely so
       its top end stays hidden, and the shoulder IS the pivot — so the concealed
       end cannot move, at any angle, and nothing can peek out from under the
       body at the bottom of the press. Translating the limb risked exactly that
       and had to be kept small to avoid it. A limb pivoting at the shoulder is
       also what a foreleg does.

       One 7s clock for all five keyframed things. On separate durations the
       press, the sweep and the tilt drift apart within a minute and the scene
       stops being one sentence. */

    /* transform-origin 0% 0% IS the shoulder, exactly. `fill-box` uses the
       OBJECT bounding box, which excludes the stroke — so for this path it is
       x[240,263] y[122,134], whose top-left corner is the path's own start
       point at (240 122). Change the `d` and this origin moves with it.

       Three fill-box origins now live in this cat (.paw, .breathe, .head). They
       are SIBLINGS, not nested, which is the distinction scene-motion.css draws:
       a transformed child inside another fill-box element moves its parent's
       bounding box and feeds back. Keep them siblings. */
    .paw {
      transform-box: fill-box;
      transform-origin: 0% 0%;
      animation: press 7s ease-in-out infinite;
    }
    @keyframes press {
      0%,
      26%,
      52%,
      100% {
        transform: rotate(0deg);
      }
      /* 6 degrees at a 26-unit radius carries the paw 2.3 units down and 1.4
         in — the old tap's travel, on an arc instead of a straight line. */
      33%,
      43% {
        transform: rotate(6deg);
      }
    }

    /* The head leans into the press, then TILTS at what the lights are doing.
       The tilt is the part that makes this read as a cat rather than a
       mechanism, and it is the one movement in the scene that is not work.
       Origin 50% 100% is the bottom centre of the head's box, which is where
       the head meets the body. .head carries no transform ATTRIBUTE — the
       static placement is on the cat's parent group — so there is nothing here
       for the animation to clobber. */
    .head {
      transform-box: fill-box;
      transform-origin: 50% 100%;
      animation: tilt 7s ease-in-out infinite;
    }
    @keyframes tilt {
      0%,
      22%,
      88%,
